A||N||31||Eriamjh||Poly Bushing Dynamics?||||5||
Z||000000||Eriamjh||11-28-2001||09:09 PM||||I know a lot of people out there have polyeurothane bushings in their Fieros, but I have this question for those engineers for experts:<P>What are the bushing dynamics?  In other words, how does the bushing react within the bolt and control arm system?  In a rubber bushing, the rubber is bonded to the inner sleeve and the outer sleeve and it twists partially with suspension travel.  But a poly bushing is far too firm to do any twisting, isn't it?<P>We'll if they don't twist, what do they do?  Does the bushing rotate with relation to the inner sleeve or the outer sleeve or does it rotate a little against both?<P>The reason I ask this is I am installing polys in my fronts of my '87 and the upper bushings slipped into the sleeves quite easily with very little effort.  I didn't need a hammer or nothing, just used my hand!<P>There doesn't seem to be any play, but I'm concerned it's not a tight enough fit.  Should I be concerned?  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Jefrysuko||11-28-2001||10:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Eriamjh:<BR><B>But a poly bushing is far too firm to do any twisting, isn't it?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No it can twist just not as much as the original rubber can.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>Does the bushing rotate with relation to the inner sleeve or the outer sleeve or does it rotate a little against both?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes the bushing rotates in respect to the inner and outer sleeves.  Whichever sleeve offers the least resistance will have the most rotation<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>The reason I ask this is I am installing polys in my fronts of my '87 and the upper bushings slipped into the sleeves quite easily with very little effort.  I didn't need a hammer or nothing, just used my hand!<P>There doesn't seem to be any play, but I'm concerned it's not a tight enough fit.  Should I be concerned?  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That is perfect.  You want it loose enough to allow free rotation but not so loose that there is excess play.<P>Just a side note:  The reason poly squeaks is because they rotate against those sleeves.  The reason rubber bushings don't squeak is because there is nothing rotating against another surface.  Make sure you grease everything up real good and your friend should have lots of fun with his new found handling.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fierobruiser||11-28-2001||10:35 PM||||I've seen graphite impregnated(black) poly bushings advertised although I don't think I've seen them for Fieros. Am I correct in assuming that the red poly bushings need lubrication when they are installled and then later at some future date also need to be relubed? Do the graphite polys need the same lube or are they install and forget?<P>------------------<BR>Notchbacks Rule<BR>85GT(show car)<BR>88Coupe (daily driver)<BR>84 Coupe(project car)||||reg||1
Z||000003||West Coast Fiero||11-29-2001||12:17 AM||||poly squeaks becuase it has a greater durometer factor than OEM rubber, not becuase of the way it rotates<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jefrysuko||11-29-2001||01:36 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B>poly squeaks becuase it has a greater durometer factor than OEM rubber, not becuase of the way it rotates<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That is a big word but I hate to say that it has nothing to do with why poly squeaks and rubber doesn't.  The rubber bushings are physically bonded to the inner and outer sleeves.  Therefore there is not any type of a frictional surface which could cause a resonant frequency which would be heard as a squeak.  The fact that poly bushings rotate in their sleeves this type of frictional surface is present and thus can cause said resonant frequency.<P>Yes there is some truth in what you say about the physical properties about the OEM rubber, but the way in which you discredit me indicates that you do not understand what you said and has caused you to speak out of context.  All rubber, no matter what it's physical properties are, can be induced to have a resonant frequency.  An example of this is the chirp that is present in the 88 Fiero's suspension.  There are rubber bushings that attach the sway bars to the frame.  As the sway bar rotates in these rubber bushings a resonant frequency (chirp) is produced due to the frictional surface between the bushing and the sway bar.  If the bushings were bonded to the sway bar said frictional surface would not exist and thus no resonant frequency (chirp).  I do believe that the sway bar bushings have physical properties which are much more similar to that of the OEM rubber control arm bushings than that of poly ones;  Therefore if a frictional surface did exist between the rubber control arm bushings and a rotating surface there would in fact be a resonant frequency that would most likely be heard as some type of noise that would resemble a squeak, chirp, etc.<P>And please use the spell checker next time.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Nashco||11-29-2001||02:30 AM||||I'm with Jefrysuko on this one; the stock rubber doesn't make noise because it is bonded..the only thing that absorbs shock is the compression/stretching force of the rubber.  The poly squeaks because it slides along the surface of the sleeves, and is not serviceable as they come in kit form.<P>If you're looking for a very very tight suspension and no noise, you can go with the UHMW bushings from 8shark.com, as they are silent and very hard.<P>The poly does use lube when installed, as much as can be used.  Regardless, mine still started squeaking after a month or so.  Some say their squeaking goes away...but I tend to believe this is because it starts wearing material away and getting loose without grease.  You can relube them, but only by taking them back apart as they went together and redoing it all.  If you install some sort of zirc fittings you can grease them, but not many people have had success with any of these systems.<P>Noise or no noise, I chose the poly for its greater stiffness and price compared to rubber bushings.<P>Oh, by the way, it is a toss up, some people think the poly rotates against the outer sleeve, some think the bolt sleeve rotates.  My frame tabs are a little screwed up, and they have left a strange wear mark in one of my bushings, leading me to believe the poly rotates on the inner bolt sleeve rather than the outer bushing sleeve.  If not, it does both, but definitely not just the outer.  It took a 1.5 pound rubber mallet and some coersion to get my bushings in.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||West Coast Fiero||11-29-2001||11:36 AM||||its amazing how everyone has a theory, I am not about to get into this argument so I will leave it here<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000007||Jefrysuko||11-29-2001||11:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B>its amazing how everyone has a theory, I am not about to get into this argument so I will leave it here<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You already did get yourself into this argument, in fact you caused it.  Go back and read what I wrote again and you won't see much theory most of what I said is factual.  In fact I will make it simple for you:<P>FACT:  OEM rubber bushings can not produce a resonant frequency (squeak) due to the lack of frictional surface required to create said frequency.<P>Now can you offer something constructive in order to refute this fact or are you just here because you feel like stirring things up?  I do believe it is the ladder so please consider making some statement so that not everyone else thinks so little of you!||||reg||1
Z||000008||West Coast Fiero||11-29-2001||03:06 PM||||whoa buddy - quit poking the coals<P><BR> I guess this shows that I'm just not allowed to say anything on this forum anymore, thats why I left for 2 months, proves positive that the only thing this forum is good for is starting arguments and letting a small portion of menbers hide behind the "skirt" of their computer to say anything they want<P>to some it up - " Let it go, man "<P><BR>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<p>[This message has been edited by West Coast Fiero (edited 11-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||tesmith66||11-29-2001||05:28 PM||||The rubber in an OEM bushing (which is bonded to the inner AND outer sleeves)deflects to allow the control arms to rotate about the pivot bolts. Unfortuneately, it also deflects enough to not provide any positive location of the outer sleeve, therefore the control arm can move in undesirable ways (in-out and fore-aft). These were selected by the manufacturer because they are quiet and isolate the frame from the control arm, reducing vibration and providing a smoother ride.<P>Polyurethane bushings are actually acting as bearings. They are not bonded to the inner and outer sleeves, therefore they can (and will) rotate either about the inner sleeve, or inside the outer sleeve. They deflect very little, so the control arm does not want to move in-out or fore-aft. It only allows the arm to rotate about the pivot bolts. This results in the control arm geometry (in relationship to the centerline of the car) to remain stable and improve handling. The trade-off is that the harder material transmits MUCH more vibration to the frame, resulting in a harsh ride. They will also squeak when they get dry.<P>The best way to avoid the squeak is to run a tap through the hole in the bushing (just cut threads in it like you were going to screw a bolt in) and pack the threads with teflon grease before assembly. Use a fine threaded tap for this, and be careful not to enlarge the hole diameter. Use teflon grease on the inner and outer sleeves, as well. Grease the puddin' out of it. You HAVE to use teflon grease, though. DO NOT use silicone based grease. Also, if they do start to squeak, DO NOT spray them with silicone (WD-40 or similar). They will squeak more, and then you will have to disassemble everything and re-grease them.<P>If you don't have enough teflon grease (I bought extra from the Fiero Store when I bought my bushings) you can use Reese teflon hitch ball grease from Wal-Mart. (A LOT cheaper than the ProThane stuff).<P>Good luck with the project. BTW, the best way I found to get the bushings out was to remove the control arm, leave them on the arm and burn them out with a torch Then I cleaned the inside of the outer sleeve up with a brake cylinder hone.<P>Later!<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||4
Z||000010||avengador1||11-29-2001||06:39 PM||||I think it's time for you boys to take a timeout. Right or wrong we are here to help others not to vicker amongst ourselves about trivial things.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||13
Z||000011||Eriamjh||11-29-2001||09:05 PM||||Gee, I didn't expect this many answers nor did I expect any real controversy.<P>I had no problem getting the old bushings out.  About 5 minutes heating the inner sleeve with a propane torch and it would push out with a suction "pop".  Then I pried out the rest with a screwdriver.  Some came out completely clean with no residue on the outer shell.<P>Those that had residue or didn't come out in one piece, required a little more heat, some scraping, then a good grinding out with a wire brush dremel attachment and/or a sandpaper flapdisk.||||reg||1
Z||000012||shark93726||11-29-2001||09:25 PM||||poly, steel, aluminum, or UHMW bushings all (normally) rotate around the bolts sleeve, not the outer sleeve, for two reasons.<P>One, the interference fit in the outer sleeves creates more friction there.<P>And, two, there is only about one third the surface area between the inner sleeve and bushing hole, which means it rotates easier there than at the outer diameter, again due to greater friction at the outer diameter.<P>The exception would be the ones that are too loose at the outer sleeve, as you indicated yours are.<P>All of the offset steel, aluminum, Delrin, and my UHMW polyethylene bushings use setscrews to hold the outer part of the bushing to the outer sleeve, otherwise, if it rotated at all, it would change the camber setting, not good at all.<P>In the case of concentric bushings, however, rotation at the outer sleeve would not be serious, although it could create more rapid wear and looseness.<P>If yours are truely that loose, I would suggest putting a couple of sheetmetal screws in the outer sleeves, so the bushings do not rotate except at the inner sleeve.  It can't hurt, and it may lengthen their life.<P>Hope this helps ya.<P>Gerald Storvik (8shark.com)||||reg||1
Z||000013||Dan010||11-29-2001||09:32 PM||||OK, Since all this dicussion, if one is not going to race his Fiero, which would be recommended for use in the front end then, which one in the rear of the car???<BR>Would like to rebuild my 86gt auto this winter.||||reg||1
Z||000014||2birds||11-29-2001||10:06 PM||||I have poly bushings all around, and I would recommend that setup if you are going to do it at all.  Yes, the bumps and grooves in the road are a lot more noticeable, but you DO get used to it (eventually).  I used to have terrible rear camber change, to the point that the insides of the rear tires would wear WAY faster than the insides.  Not anymore, though.  Doubled my tire life since the poly install.<BR>The squeak is virtually non-existent on mine, unless the temp is &lt;45 degrees (fahrenheidt to you Canucks).  I have also installed zerk fittings in mine, drilling all the way to the bolt bushing.  I grease them and the anti-sway bar bushings every time I change the oil, and it seems to work great.  While I'd be nervous about threading the innser surface of the bushings, I think a groove (like the ones in a quality crankshaft journal) would work great.  Didn't feel like pulling mine apart again to do it, but if I was rebuilding again I would.<P>By the way, mine's had full poly and ST springs for almost 7 years and 50,000 miles.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Jefrysuko||11-29-2001||11:51 PM||||Ok guy's sorry if I came off a little rough.  I felt as if I was being challenged by WCF and that he was wanting to start a debate.  In an e-mail he stated to me that he feels no need for him to debate on this forum.  I respect that and agree that this forum can use less of the flame wars that start out as debates.<P>So I offer a gentlemans agreement to WCF and have full intention to honor it.  If you do not feel like debating on this forum with me simply do not make any statements that contradict what I have already said.  I in return will refrain from the personal nature of my responses so that in the future we might be able to have a constructive debate.||||reg||1
Z||000016||shark93726||11-30-2001||12:07 AM||||If you are not going to race (or autocross) your Fiero at all, and are not concerned with maximum cornering on the street either, I would recommend putting in new MOOG rubber bushings, especially in the front.  They will give you a smoother ride than the solid bushings, and will probably last for at least 5-10 years.<P>The rear suspension, however, is more of a problem.  The A-arm design of the '84-'87 Fieros allows too much camber and toe change in the rear suspension with rubber bushings under aggressive cornering, braking, and accelerating.<P>Solid bushings of any type will cure this on the rear, and improve handling considerably, but will also ride a little harsher, so you have to make your choice and live with it.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I started making the offset UHMW polyethylene bushings thirty years ago for a C-Prepared Camaro Z-28, and then just recently made some more for my Fiero, because no one offered offset ones that gave me the camber and caster adjustment I wanted.<P>I then decided to offer them to other Fiero owners.  They are a little more expensive to make than the polyurethane ones, but the double offsets make them worth it to me at least. (plus they don't squeak like the polyurethane ones do.)<P>You can get more details about them off my web site (shameless plug here) at <A HREF="http://www.8shark.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.8shark.com/</A> <P>But if you are not into performance driving I would still recommend Moog or other replacement rubber bushings. <P>Gerald Storvik||||reg||1
Z||000017||theogre||11-30-2001||07:56 PM||||In general, I don't ever recomend anything but OE style rubber bushings in the front of a RWD vehicle. The rears may need polly or something even stiffer depending both on how you drive and what motor is in it.<P>shark, read most of your site today. very interesting stuff. I had some thoughts about that rear alignment problem I'll email later...<P>How polly works....<P>It's mainly a bearing when used in suspension parts. It should pivot only around the inner sleeve. If it's pivoting against the outter sleeve something is wrong.<P>In sway bar ends or pillows it's effectivly the same as rubber but stiffer.<P>Greasing polly....<P>Put the bushing in the outer shell. Before installing the inner sleeve, drill a hole straight thru the shell to the bushing center hole. tap the hole for a zirk fitting and install the fitting. (Locate the hole where you can get at it once the A arm is installed and where it won't get hit.)<P>Get one of those cheap little grease guns you can get at Wallmart or almost anyplace else. Get the flex hose for it. Load it with teflon grease. <P>You'll never have to fart with the bushings again. You'll be able to regrease them just like the ball joints.<P>Threading to retain grease....<P>be carefull with that. Done wrong it can increase slop in the bushing even if the hole seems the same.<P>IMHO Better to treat it more like rifling a gun barrel. Leave as much flat in there as you can. That would mean the most coarse tap you can find that just bites the walls of the hole. The scratch you get will hold some lube but you'll still have plenty of land to take the load.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000018||lowCG||11-30-2001||08:32 PM||||Yeah,my first Fiero had UHMW in the rear control arms that I made;worked incredible,the car actually rode better because the energy from hitting bumps was being transmitted to the shock/spring,not getting the delayed little thump from a squishy,rubber isolator.<BR>I have poly now(in back),as I didn't know about shark's deal when I put my car together.(where were 'ya man?)<BR>From looking at the urethane,it's kind of "mealy",has grain,and I can imagine that it wouldn't wear very well compared to greased UHMW.<BR>I used Moog rubber in front,and don't have any problems with handling,even with the new bigger brakes,but I'm going to keep an eye on how the tires wear from this point on also.<BR>Shark,have you tried any caster in the back,by slotting the upper strut mounting holes?<BR>A little adjustment(3/8")can give an improvement in how the car reacts to bumps at speed. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by lowCG (edited 11-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||shark93726||12-01-2001||01:57 AM||||You mean extra camber on the back, right?<P>Yeah, the test car has poly bushings in it from before I made the UHMW ones, so I had to slot the three strut mount holes to get a little extra camber on the rear. I didn't figure it was worth the work to change the poly to UHMW, since I am already building the modified car and will probably not be autocrossing the test one much more at all.<P>I ended up with aboout 2.5 degrees negative camber on the rear.  Works really great.  Almost perfectly neutral handling with no rear bar.<P>I am also VERY pleased with removal of steering stabilizer, feels like I added power steering.  I am now going to try to find one of the quicker ratio racks from a junkyard to put on the modified car.<P>Gerald Storvik (8shark.com)||||reg||1
Z||000020||westtexas||12-01-2001||07:47 AM||||This is a fantastic thread! Learning from the experts. I haven't used the bushing I bought from Shark on Ebay but he ships quick. They look extremely well made. One of my cars is going to have an all-out, no-compromise suspension and this thread will be a road map. Thanks!!!||||reg||1
Z||000021||lowCG||12-01-2001||11:02 AM||||Nope,caster,only effective in small amounts,but I swear it made a difference.<BR>This kind of setup is seen more in smaller race cars(open-wheel,F1,cart,formula junior,etc)<BR>Also,have you turned the upper strut mounting upside-down;notice the recess' in the underside of the rear hatch,allowing you to do this(of course you usually get a little interference after moving the strut mounting rearward a little).This gives some more travel back there,howver,you'll need to locate the spring somehow.<BR>I've never even messed with the steering stabilizer in all my trial and error,Guess I'll try removing it,and if it's not blown out already,see what that's like.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000022||Fierowrecker||12-01-2001||11:29 AM||||Hey WCF!<BR>If you have an opinion, I would like to hear it!<BR>I have poly on the front only on my current Fiero, and I definately plan on adding it to the rear...<BR>My last Fiero, I added poly all around, and it was great...<BR>My wife's Fiero is stock rubber, and you can tell very easily after a turn or two...<BR>I have heard that aluminum is better for the engine cradle, but won't it wear away?<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000023||shark93726||12-01-2001||01:23 PM||||To Fierowrecker:<P>Once you bolt up aluminum (or anything else that hard) to the rear cradle, it doesn't move at all, so there should be absolutely no wear, unless you don't get the bolts tight enough.<P>To lowCG:<P>Very interesting, hadn't even considered changing the caster on the rear suspension. <P>If I were going to continue working on the '87 test car, that might be fun to try.  <P>The new '84 Modified car will no longer have McPherson struts (eventually) though, so that won't be applicable on it.  I am planning on custom building a custom rear suspension, combining a DeDion design with a Watts link for lateral control, which should give perfect camber at all times.  If I can make it fit with the stock cradle, I may even offer it for sale, or at least publish pictures and specs on my web site, so others can build their own if desired.<P>Gerald Storvik (8shark.com)||||reg||1
Z||000024||westtexas||12-01-2001||07:18 PM||||On my '87 coupe, if I use a Held bumpsteer kit, aluminum bushings on the cradle, and Sharks bushing the rear, would that be worse, equal, or better than an '88 suspension as far as keeping the wheels pointed in the right direction? I'm guessing that would cost about $1000. Are the '88's worth a $1000 more as far as handling goes? If I was to transplant a '88 cradle in the '87, then I would have to use coil overs which also cost $$$. I know this is bending the thread to a little different subject but I sure need your expertise. I may just use the '87 as a parts car. My '88 does have a loose right front wheel bearing. Will the '88 need suspension/brake/??? parts that I can't get?<P>Thanks, I need your help on  deciding on which car to work on, <BR>Robert<p>[This message has been edited by westtexas (edited 12-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||shark93726||12-02-2001||06:15 AM||||An '84-'87 with bumpsteer and solid rear bushings throughout will be a little better than an '88 with oem rubber bushings, but the '88 would be about equal with solid bushings added on the rear.<P>While the Lotus designed '88 rear is much better than the older oem design, it still does have a small amount of deflection from cornering forces etc., and can also be improved with solid bushings. <P>Of course, both will end up with a firmer ride that way, so the '88 rear with rubber bushings is a better compromise between handling and comfort, just dont be surprised if you get beat in an autocross by Fieros with all solid bushings.<P>The simple rule is:  The less deflection you can engineer into a suspension, (and frame) the better the handling capability will be, assuming the proper alignment settings are set.<P>Gerald Storvik (8shark.com)||||reg||1
Z||000026||Phil||12-02-2001||08:43 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><P>........While the Lotus designed '88 rear is much better than the older oem design, it still does have a small amount of deflection from cornering forces etc., and can also be improved with solid bushings...........<P><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Let me be the first to say that Lotus had nothing to do with the design of the 88 Fiero suspension.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Phil (edited 12-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000027||shark93726||12-02-2001||01:39 PM||||Really?<P>Could be...<P>I have seen so many quotes about Lotus designig it that I just assumed it was fact.<P>I stand corrected then.<P>Thanks,<P>Gerald Storvik (8shark.com)||||reg||1
Z||000028||Standard||12-02-2001||04:35 PM||||All that lotus stuff is just a rumour.  The '88 front and rear suspension design is what was actually supposed to go on the Fiero from the start, before the beancounters got to it.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">          <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000029||dourain86gt||12-04-2001||02:26 AM||||Wow! Am I really confused! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"><BR>I was very much considering bushings but after reading all this... I have no Idea which ones to get. I don't race my car but I love tight suspension, but i also don't like my fillings falling out either as my car is a daily driver... so help. 8ball Ithink I might go with your set up but to install with all this camber and toe talk I am a little afraid. You have very reasonable prices which I like but complicated adjustments which I personaly don't understand... yet anyway. And complicated usually means expensive install.<P>help!||||reg||1
Z||000030||ThatRickGuy||12-04-2001||05:52 PM||||I'm running poly's up front and OEM rubber in the rear on my '88, mainly due to the finances of the whole thing. I can definately feel the front bite in on the corners, and I have a lot more of a loose feeling on the car (as in tight front, loose rear). A good set of struts and some Delrins on the rear will probrably take car of that though. And for the whole lotus thing, I beleive the rumor was started after someone knowticed the similarityies between some of the design on the 88 and a vehicle from lotus, some people claimed that this was because GM had purchased a lotus car and used it as a model. or, it was just a by chance resemblence, I mean, why buy a $250,000 car, to make similar suspension, and the put rubber in for the bushings?!?||||reg||1
Z||000031||shark93726||12-04-2001||09:42 PM||||To Dourain86gt:<P>QUOTE: Ithink I might go with your set up but to install with all this camber and toe talk I am a little afraid. You have very reasonable prices which I like but complicated adjustments which I personaly don't understand... yet anyway. And complicated usually means expensive install.<BR>_____________________________________________<P>I am inserting my instructions for front upper A-arm bushings, and also my alignment guide.  That should give you a better idea what the install is like, as well as hopefully simplifying the alignment settings and adjustment procedure.  Hope this helps ya.<P>8Shark Fiero ‘84-’87 front Upper A-arm Instructions:<P>TOOLS NEEDED:<BR>1 Floor jack<BR>4 adjustable jack stands (need range of aprox. 10-16”)<BR>1 7/16” box or open end wrench (for new brake line clamp bolts)<BR>1 7/16” 3/8” drive socket (for new brake line clamp bolts)<BR>1 13mm. box or open-end wrench<BR>1 13mm. 3/8” drive socket<BR>1 15mm. box or open-end wrench<BR>1 15mm. 3/8” drive socket<BR>1 18mm. box or open-end wrench<BR>1 18mm. 6-point ½” drive socket<BR>1 19mm. (or ¾”) ½” drive socket (for removing lug nuts, or you can use lug wrench)<BR>1 3/8” drive ratchet<BR>1 3/8” drive 6” extension<BR>1 ½” drive ratchet<BR>1 ½” breaker-bar <BR>1 ½” drive 6” extension<BR>1 ¼” drill bit<BR>1 1/8” drill bit<BR>1 ¼” or 3/8” drill<BR>1 phillips screwdriver<BR>1 1” wire wheel with ¼” shaft<BR>1 wire brush wheel (6-8” diameter, mounted on stationary grinder)<BR>1 ball-joint pickle fork<BR>1 needle nose pliers<BR>1 hammer (at least 20 oz., 3 pound sledge would be better)<BR>1 pair 12” adjustable (channel lock) pliers<BR>2 new cotter pins (for the ball joint nuts)<BR>1 wire coathanger or some tie wire<BR>1 work light<BR>1 box to hold all hardware (so you don’t lose any of it)<BR>1 or 2 friends to help (several more to give advice)<P><BR>STEP 1: REMOVING A-arms<BR>SPECIAL NOTE:  Remember, do NOT push too hard sideways in any direction on the car, you could  cause the jack stands to tip and crush one of your friends, and you may need them to help finish.<BR>1:  Jack up front of car about 8-12” and put jack stands under each side of the front suspension frame cross member, then lower floor jack till car is firmly on jack stands. (we are doing this in stages to minimize the chance of tipping the car off the jack stands )<BR>2:  Jack up rear of car about 16-18” and put jack stands under each side of rear sub frame near back edge where emergency brake cable passes through, then lower jack till car is firmly on jack stands.<BR>3:  jack front rest of the way up to 16-18” and extend front jack stands, then lower jack till car is firmly on jack stands.<BR>4:  Remove lug nuts from front wheels and remove front wheels. (One of the friends can sit in car and hold brake while you loosen lug nuts with breaker bar and/or ratchet.)<BR>5:  Place floor jack under one A-arm with wood block crossways to the A-arm (helps keep the A-arm from sliding off of jack) and jack it up just till you see the upper A-arm lift up a little, then use 15mm. and 13mm. tools to remove bottom and top bolts from shock absorber.  Remove shock, then repeat for other side.<BR>6:  Remove nuts from both upper A-arm inner pivot bolts, do NOT remove bolts at this time.<BR>7. Use small drill bit to start a hole in the center of the rivet that holds the brake line clamp to the A-Arm, then use the ¼” bit to drill out rivet.  (You can use a screwdriver or small chisel to knock clamp loose if the rivet doesn’t drill completely out) <BR>8:  Next turn steering wheel to get clearance on the side you are working on, then remove cotter pin from upper ball joint on that side, (use needle nose pliers and/or awl/nail to get it out) <BR>9:  Loosen upper ball-joint nut ONLY about 2 turns so it is still fully on threads but has about 1/16” gap under it.<BR>10:  Drive pickle fork in between rubber boot and upper knuckle (below rubber) with hammer until it pops tapered stud loose.  Remove castle nut and pull stud out of knuckle.  (Be careful not to kink or stress brake line)  You can use a piece of tie-wire from spring to hold knuckle roughly in place. <BR>11:  Tap on end of long bolt to push it forward and out of inner A-Arm pivot bushings. (There should be a thick washer on the inside edge of each bushing, and one or two thin washers on the outside of each bushing)  Once the long bolt clears the tube in the frame, the A-Arm should come free and can be removed.  Then slowly lower floor jack until free. <BR>12:  Repeat steps 5 through 11 for other side of car.<P><BR>STEP 2: REMOVING Rubber and bolt tubes from A-arms<BR>1:  If you have access to a hydraulic press, you can press the center tubes out of the A-arms first, (be careful not to deform the A-arm sheet metal where it has the sleeve pressed through it) otherwise, the rubber can be drilled out or burned out with a torch (butane or acetylene)  These center tubes will be re-used for your new bushings, so clean any rust or rubber left on them with a stationary wire brush wheel.<BR>2:  If the center tubes were pressed out, you can then use water pump pliers to grab the rubber grommet and pry it out of the outer steel sleeves.  NOTE: Sometimes the rubber is vulcanized (stuck) to the outer sleeve and/or to the center tube., in which case it may have to be burnt or cut out.  The remaining bits of rubber stuck in the sleeve can be cleaned out by using a 1” diameter wire wheel attached to a small drill.<BR>3:  At this point, consider replacing the ball-joints on the A-arms. (They probably have 15 years of wear, and the grease boots may be split open)  You can unbolt them and obtain new Moog ball-joints from a Napa store.  These will then be bolted in. (These top ball joints are slightly offset, and you can flip them around 180 degrees to change camber adjustment about 1 degree.  Make sure you take this into account when you decide how much camber you are trying to get, so you don’t have to do it over later.  They can be unbolted and revolved without removing anything else, however.)<BR>____________________________________________________________________________________________<BR>STEP 3: INSTALLING Poly bushings in A-arms<BR>NOTE:  If you intend to use offset bushings on the lower A-arms also, and/or turn the top ball joints around which adds about 1 degree of negative camber, you may not need the full 1/2 degree of negative camber available with these upper bushings, and can then change the installation to add about 1 degree more caster from the upper bushings.  The way to do this is to install the FRONT bushing in your upper A-arms as stated below, with the center hole AWAY from the ball-joint, but install the REAR bushings with the center hole rotated 180 degrees CLOSEST to the ball joint. Since the two bushings and ball-joint form a triangle, if the Front bushing is pushed out, and the rear bushing is pushed in, you will push the ball joint rearward instead of outward, which would result in approximately zero change in camber, but close to 1 degree of caster added.  It is important to decide what total camber you are trying to arrive at, and set the upper and lower A-Arm bushings and the offset upper ball joints so that you will obtain that goal.  (Refer to the alignment section to see out recommendations for camber on the Fiero)<BR>SECOND NOTE:  DO NOT press the bushings in by pressing on one end against other end of A-arm, you will BEND the A-arms doing this.  Instead, set only the one Bushing sleeve over a surface like a table top with the other end of the A-arm extending under the table top.  You can duplicate this on a press by having a strong flat bar held up by blocks at each side to leave room for the other half of the A-arm to hang below. <BR>1. Check the small end of each steel sleeve where it goes through the A-arm to make sure the edge of the hol;e in the A-arm doesn’t stick out past the sleeve.  If it does, file or grind off the excess so it doesn’t interfere with the flange on the poly bushing ring when you install them. <BR>NOTE:  There are four different sizes of bushing halves in this set, (two of each size)  To make sure they all get installed in the correct position, sort them this way.  The longest halves and the shortest halves go together on the FRONT sleeve of the A-Arm, with the thick flanged half at the front, and the thin flanged half at the rear of the sleeve. (you will notice that the turned down part on one half is slightly bigger in diameter than the other half, and this bigger diameter half must go in the flanged end of the sleeve)  The remaining two pairs of buhings halves go in the rear sleeve of the A-Arms, again with the thick flanged end towards the front, and the thin flanged end towards the rear of the car, and the bigger diameter half goes in the flanged end of the steel sleeve.  You can determine which is front and back of  the A-Arm by noting that the two ¼” holes that the brake line clamp fastened to are to the REAR half of the A-Arm.  MAKE SURE YOU GET THIS RIGHT, BECAUSE IT IS NOT EASY TO GET THEM OUT AND DO IT OVER.<BR>2:  Align each bigger diameter half of the Poly bushing with the hole offset AWAY from the ball-joint, or if desired, with the FRONT one AWAY, and REAR one rotated 180 degrees CLOSEST to the ball joint. (the steel sleeves in the A-arms have a flange on one end, and the bushing inserts from that end)  Either press or hammer in the bigger diameter end using a flat plate or other suitable piece so you don’t deform the bushing or the A-arm.<BR>3:  Press or hammer in the center tubes in each bushing.  If it makes you feel better you can put some grease on these, but I have not found it to be necessary.<BR>4:  Press the smaller half bushing over the steel tube and into the other end of each steel sleeve, aligning the hole to match the other bushing half, again using a flat plate to keep from damaging the flanged ring.  You can insert the long bolt through the other bushing against the flat plate to press or tap the bushing over the steel tube and into the steel sleeve.<BR>5:  Use the 1/8” drill bit to drill  a hole and a phillips screwdriver to drive a screw into each steel sleeve to hold larger diameter bushing half in place. (put screws on opposite side from offset hole in bushing so they clear center tube)<BR>______________________________________________________________________________________________<BR>STEP 4: RE-INSTALLING A-arms on Car<BR>SPECIAL NOTE:  Remember, do NOT push too hard sideways in any direction on the car, you could  cause the jack stands to tip and crush one of your friends, and you may need them to help finish.<BR>1:  Put one or two (whatever originally came on it ) of the thin washers on the long bolt and insert the long bolt through the FRONT bushing of the A-Arm, so it sticks out about ¼”.  Put ONE of the new hardened washers on the end of the bolt with the flat side towards the bushing flange.  Next, set the A-arm back into place and tap or push the bolt through just even with the back edge of the tube in the frame.  While holding THREE of the new hardened washers in place with the flat sides towards the rear bushing flange, (you can wrap them with a piece of electrical tape to make it easier to hold them all together) tap or push the long bolt all the way through the washers and the rear bushing.  Add the other thin original washer(s) on the bolt and put the nut on and tighten to the correct torque. <BR>2:  Lift the A-Arm up a ways and use the new bolt and nylon lock nut to fasten the brake line clamp in place in the rear hole.  (make sure you are not kinking or stressing brake line)<BR>3:  Place floor jack under outer end of lower A-arm and raise it up a ways, until you can set spindle knuckle over ball-joint stud and screw nut on stud (if possible, first rotate stud so cotter pin hole goes sideways to spindle, this makes it easier to insert and fold cotter pin later) <BR>4:  Tighten nut to specified torque, then get it to align with cotter pin hole and install and fold cotter pin.<BR>5: repeat steps 1 through 4 for other side of car.<BR>FINISH:<BR>1:  Jack up each A-arm slightly and re-install shock absorbers.<BR>2:  Install wheels and tighten to proper torque.<BR>3:  Reverse your original jacking procedure to lower the car back to ground safely.<BR>4:  Roll or drive car back and forth 20-30 feet while pushing down on front of car to settle suspension.<BR>5:  Check alignment and adjust toe-in of front wheels. (this will probably be off due to changes in alignment from bushings)<BR>6:  Test drive car.  Re-check toe-in and re-adjust if necessary.<BR>______________________________________________________________________________________<P><BR>ALIGNMENT RECOMMENDATIONS: (With all solid bushings installed)<P>Remember, the overall most important rule in setting up a suspension for MAXIMUM handling is simply to keep the biggest possible patch of each tire "connected" to the the road under all driving conditions.  This, in turn, requires that each tire be as nearly vertical to the ground as possible under all conditions, and that each tire/wheel have the "softest" possible suspension rate under loaded cornering, braking, and accelerating conditions.<P>All Street-driven high performance tires with standard front anti sway bar (no rear bar):  For even wear and minimum scuffing of outer edge of tread with “spirited” driving, you will want about 1-1/2 degrees of negative camber and all the caster you can get on the front suspension. (6-8 degrees is a good range to shoot for)  The idea here is to allow the car to lean more on hard corners but correct the suspension alignment so the tires are still vertical to the ground.  The rear suspension needs about the same 1-1/2 degrees of camber.  Toe should be set as near to zero as possible in the front for minimum tire wear in highway driving, but never toed in, with more toe-out reducing “dartiness” in the front steering feel.  Rear toe can be set between zero and 1/8” toe-in, never toed out, again, keeping it nearer zero for minimum wear, and more toe-in for less “spin-out” tendancy. If you are unwilling to live with the "firmer" ride of solid bushings, or occasionally want to autocross in Stock class where you are not allowed to use solid bushings, you will need to add another 1/2 degree of negative camber both front and rear to compensate.  And you should consider replacing all of the suspension bushings with new Moog or similar ones to minimize give.  Since it is nearly impossible to get that much camber and caster with stock rubber bushings, a larger front sway bar would be advisable.  The reduction in body roll will require a little less camber to achieve good handling.<P>AUTOCROSS Stock and Street-Prepared - Kumho and Hoosier (and all similar) D.O.T. Racing tires with standard front anti sway bar:  The major difference here is that the D.O.T. Racing tires generate significantly more cornering forces than regular high performance street tires, which means a larger amount of body roll.  Therefore, you would want to have about another ½ degree of camber at both ends of the car, (about 2 degrees) and the 1/16” front toe-out and 1/8” rear toe-in become more important for maximum performance.  Also, any lowering of the car will help here, either by smaller diameter tires, or by lowering the springs. (be sure to cut the bump stops some if you lower the springs though, because hitting a bump stop in the middle of a corner will result in sudden loss of traction on that wheel)  Again, the idea here is to keep the suspension soft and allow the car to lean more on hard corners but correct the suspension alignment so the tires are still vertical to the ground. In Stock class, however, you are not allowed to use solid bushings, and therefore, will have to add another 1/2 degree of negative camber both front and rear to compensate.  And you should consider replacing all of the suspension bushings with new Moog or similar ones to minimize give.  Since it is nearly impossible to get that much camber and caster with stock rubber bushings, a larger front sway bar would be advisable for Stock class.  The reduction in body roll will require a little less camber to achieve good handling.<P>AUTOCROSS - Full Racing Tires:  Again, the major difference here is that full racing tires (usually on wider rims with more tread width) will generate more cornering force than the D.O.T. racing tires.  Usually, this will finally require the use of a larger front anti sway bar, and possibly, the addition of a small rear anti sway bar, in order to keep the body roll from extremes.  Extra lowering of the car can help alleviate the body roll somewhat also.  Again, though, the goal is to keep the suspension as soft as possible for maximum traction.  Ideally, we do not want to “eliminate” body roll, we would just like to limit the body roll to about the same as experienced with D.O.T. Racing tires.  The recommended starting point would be a 1” aftermarket front bar on the  ‘84-’87 Fieros, without a rear bar. (a rear bar loses a lot of traction on the inside tire on acceleration, not desirable)  The camber should be about 2 degrees negative both front and rear, and the toe should be the same as with D.O.T. racing tires.  Front caster should be about the same also.  If the front cornering seems significantly “looser” than the rear with the 1” front bar, then a small rear bar might help to “balance” the car, but will then require a more “subtle” accelerator application to keep from breaking the rear tires loose. (or a lot more steering correction to keep the car going in a forward direction)<P>NOTE:   A little known fact is that the "stiffness" of a anti sway bar, (measured in the foot pounds of torque required to deflect it an equal amount) increases in proportion to the FOURTH power of the increase in diameter.  So, for instance, if we increase the diameter of an anti sway bar from 7/8" to 1", we have a "stiffness" factor of 7/8 X 7/8 X 7/8 X 7/8, or 0.586 for the 7/8" bar, and 1.000 for the 1" bar, which means that the 1" bar is 1/.586, or over 70% stiffer.  That is a LOT of increase.  Even a 1/16" increase in an anti sway bar diameter will increase stiffness about 35%.<P><BR>DESCRIPTIONS:<P>TOE-IN:  Toe is typically measured by the amount in fractions of an inch that each wheel is turned in or out from being parallel to the centerline of car.  This is done by measuring the difference in distance between the front and back edge of the rim from a bar held parallel to the cars centerline.  The desirable condition is to have zero toe, but if any other conditions of suspension give or bump steer change the geometry, it is better to err on the side of toe-out rather than toe-in, since toe-in on  the front will result in the car being “darty”, an unstable condition, and breaking loose or losing traction easier, where toe-out will only result in the car turning slightly less for a given steering input then it would with zero toe, which just means you have to turn the steering wheel slightly more than would otherwise be needed.<P>Toe on the rear of a car with independent rear suspension like the Fiero, should be exactly the opposite of front toe, for several reasons.  It should never be toed-out, because this will have a strong tendency to make the rear of the car spin out under hard throttle in any amount of turn.  A slight amount of toe-in at the rear will allow more throttle application in turns without the rear “drifting” out so much, and will also make the car less inclined to “come around” when braking hard up into a corner (what the infamous Porsche likes to do).<P>CAMBER:  Camber is simply the amount the top of the rim leans inward toward the center of the car. (inward at the top is called negative camber, and outward would be called positive camber)  If you want a quick check for camber, you can use a vertical level.  Park the car on a level spot and make sure the wheels are straight ahead.  Measure the distance of the top edge of the rim and the bottom edge of the rim from the level.  Each ¼” further from the level the top edge is than the bottom edge equals about one degree of negative camber.<P>CASTER:  Caster is the amount the top spindle ball joint leans toward the back of the car compared to the bottom spindle ball joint.  (similar to the amount of backward tilt you see on the shaft that holds the front wheel assembly on a bike or motorcycle)  Unlike camber, the top leaning backward is called positive caster.   A fairly close method to measure caster is to take a camber reading with the wheel/tire turned 14 degrees left, then another camber reading with wheel/tire turned 14 degrees right.  The difference between these two readings is the amount of caster on that wheel.  14 degrees of turn on the wheel would be about 4.5 inches difference between the front and back edge of the rim measured from a bar that is parallel to the centerline of car.<P>The reason caster is needed is because all cars made for street use (not all out racing) come with the top ball joint on the spindle angled inward towards the center of the car, most by as much as 8 degrees or more!  This is done partly to make the pivot point at the road near the center of the tire, so it doesn’t jerk the wheel out of your hands with every little bump, and partly because the tire will lean the wrong way (on purpose) in a hard turn and lose traction, so the car doesn’t turn too hard and spin out, or turn so hard that it rolls over rather than slide, both of which have gotten some car companies sued by drivers dumb enough to  do that.  <P>You can visualize this effect if you fold a piece of paper into a triangle, hold a pencil level along the top edge, pretending the pencil is the center spindle that the wheel spins on, the vertical side of the paper represents the vertical plane the wheel is spinning in, and the diagonal represents the angle between the two ball joints.  If you hold the two diagonal corners of the paper and rotate it around them forward (representing turning the wheel) the pencil will turn as the spindle would, but you will see that it no longer will be level parallel to the ground, it will have angled downward at the same time due to the angle of the ball joints.  This tilts the wheel outward at the top so the tire ends up riding on the outside edge of the tread instead of staying vertical for maximum traction. (this example exaggerates this, but the effect is the same)  If you tilt the top of the paper diagonal backwards towards you the same amount it leans inward, and start with the pencil level and sideways, now, when you rotate the paper and pencil forward around the diagonal pivot point, the pencil will remain level for a large amount of the rotation.  So ideally, the correct amount of backward lean or caster on a front suspension would be the same amount that the ball joints lean in by (this used to be called kingpin angle on old cars that actually pivoted on a shaft rather than using ball joints, trucks with leaf spring front ends still use a kingpin shaft to hold the spindle)<P>BUMP STEER:  This refers to the fact that if a wheel hits a large bump and goes up in the wheel well while turning, does it stay turned the same amount, or does it turn more, or less, than the initial amount of turning?  This is due to miss-alignment of steering geometry (such as the height of the rack and pinion or steering knuckle on spindles)  This can be checked best by placing car on jack stands, having front suspension assembled without springs installed and raising and lowering each front wheel with floor jack throughout range and measuring any change in toe.  On Fieros, the rear can bump steer also, so there are Four wheels to check.  There is no point in checking or changing bump steer until you have the full geometry set, as every other change to caster or camber will change bump steer, but it is quite important to check and adjust bump steer to zero for any race car, because a small bump that changes the direction that wheel is turned to will result in a momentary loss in traction, (like jerking the steering wheel slightly) which is very undesirable.<P>BUMP STEER ADJUSTMENT:  There are five possible conditions of bump steer, only one of which is correct.  They can be represented best for this discussion by the following symbols:  -),  -(, -|, -\, -/.  The - in each case represents a line pointing to the center of the car and the other part of the symbol represents which way the wheel turns above and below its normal ride height.  Only the third, -||, means the wheel has little or no change in turn when moved above or below its normal ride height.<P>The fourth, -\, represents the tire turning more when raised and less when lowered from its normal ride height.<P>The fifth, -/, represents the tire/wheel turning less when raised and more when lowered from its normal ride height.<P>NOTE:  Keep in mind that there could be some combination of the first two and last two conditions, which could require two separate adjustments to correct for.<P>The fourth and fifth conditions are usually easier to correct, and are also the first ones you should adjust for if you intend to bump steer the front suspension. <P>The fourth condition, -\, results from the ball joint at the end of the tie rod being too high compared to the pivot on the rack and pinion steering (or conversely, the rack and pinion being mounted too low compared to the end ball joint)  There are two ways to correct this, lower the ball joint, or raise the rack and pinion.  Since lowering the ball joint would require heating and bending the steering knuckle on the spindle, (difficult to do accurately and safely) it should be far easier, on a Fiero, to raise the rack and pinion slightly by slotting the holes it is bolted to the frame through.<P>The fifth condition, -/, is exactly the opposite of the fourth condition, and would require lowering the rack and pinion where it mounts to the frame.<P>If, after correcting the -/ and/or -\ conditions, you still have some of the first or second conditions, -) or -(, these result from the distance between the pivot at the rack and pinion and the end ball joint being too short or too long respectively.  The only way to correct this on a Fiero is either to find a different rack and pinion that is wider or narrower than the stock one, or to bend the steering knuckle at the wheel spindle outward or inward, then adjusting the tie rod shorter or longer until the wheel has the right toe again.  You can determine quite accurately how much the knuckle needs to be bent by marking the setting you currently have for the tie rod, then adjusting the tie rod longer or shorter until the bump steer comes closest to zero change when raising or lowering that wheel, and noting the amount you had to change the tie rod adjustment and which direction.  That is the direction and amount the knuckle needs to be bent. (if the tie rod had to be lengthened, you will want to bend the knuckle that much away from the rack and pinion, toward the wheel, and if the tie rod had to be shortened, you would want to bend the knuckle toward the tie rod and away from the wheel) The knuckle just needs to be heated with a torch as far as possible from the end (near where it comes off spindle) until it glows red, and carefully bent and allowed to cool slowly.  These spindles are forged steel, and oversized for extra strength, so this will not appreciably weaken them, if done correctly.  You do not want to quench or cool them quickly, because this hardens the metal and makes it brittle, which could make it break under stress like hitting a curb.  (it is much safer to have a suspension component bend rather than actually break, especially the steering parts) <P>The exact same method can be applied to bump steer the back suspension on a Fiero, and may be easier to do because you can shorten or lengthen the rear tie rod as needed by moving the mount on the frame, as well as raising or lowering it there, so no heating or bending of the steering knuckle on the spindle should be needed.<P><BR>RECOMMENDED READING AND REFERENCES:<P>“The Unfair Advantage” - by Mark Donohue, with Paul Van Valkenburgh<P>“Chevrolet=Racing...? Fourteen Years of Raucous Silence!!” - by Paul Van Valkenburgh<P>	(Both Donohue and Valkenbugh were engineers at Chevrolet )<P>“Racing and Sports Car Chassis Design” - by Michael Costin and David Phipps<P>“The Sports Car: Its Design and Performance” - by Colin Campbell<P>“Chevrolet Power” -  by Chevrolet motor Division, GMC, 3rd edition (or later)<BR>	(This has a very complete Vehicle Dynamics section that is extremely helpful)<P><BR>Gerald Storvik (8shark.com)||63.196.113.82||reg||1
